# SHELL SCRIPTS ARE LF, ALWAYS.
#
# A .sh checked out with CRLF is not a slow script or a subtly wrong one -- it does not run at
# all. bash reads `set -euo pipefail<CR>` and reports "set: pipefail: invalid option name", and
# the carriage return rewinds the cursor mid-line so the terminal shows a fragment like
# "lin: invalid option name" instead. All four scripts in this repository were in that state,
# including scripts/_prodRelease.run.sh, and none had ever been runnable from a Windows checkout.
#
# Every consumer of these files is bash. There is no platform on which CRLF is the right
# checkout, so this is not a preference -- it is the only working setting.
*.sh text eol=lf

# FIRESTORE RULES ARE LF, ALWAYS -- BECAUSE THE DEPLOYED BYTES ARE THESE BYTES.
#
# `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules` uploads the file exactly as it sits on disk. A Windows
# checkout materializes CRLF, so a Windows-originated deploy publishes a CRLF ruleset while the
# governed SHA pins Git's LF bytes -- and the deployment verifier then reports LIVE != GOVERNED for
# a ruleset that is character-for-character correct. Measured on eos-platform-sandbox 2026-08-27:
# zero line-by-line differences, and the verifier still refused.
#
# The verifier now normalizes newlines before comparing (firestoreDeploymentVerificationShared.js's
# normalizeRulesNewlines), which fixes the comparison. This forces the other half: what gets
# deployed IS the governed byte sequence, so live and governed agree without needing to be forgiven
# for anything. Both governed copies are covered -- they are required to stay byte-identical, and a
# rule covering only one would make that impossible on a Windows checkout.
firestore.rules text eol=lf
field-ops-app-vite/firestore.rules text eol=lf

